# The ledger of `service`: what a program can do as a Windows service, one block
# per capability, and what Rebel answers. The census, each list read on
# 2026-11-23: Go's `golang.org/x/sys/windows/svc` (pkg.go.dev, the package's
# index and its constants), the yardstick for Go, which has no service
# package in its standard library; Rust's `windows-service` 0.8.1 (docs.rs:
# `service`, `service_control_handler`, `service_dispatcher`), the crate the
# `rust` lines name, the standard library having none; Python's standard
# library has no service either, so the `python` lines are `-` (the
# third-party pywin32's `win32serviceutil` is the usual way, read by no list
# here). The system's own protocol, Microsoft's reference pages for
# `StartServiceCtrlDispatcherW`, `RegisterServiceCtrlHandlerExW`,
# `SetServiceStatus`, `HandlerEx` and `ServiceMain`, read the same day, is what
# `runtime/platform/hero_service.c` follows. Only Windows has services: every
# other system answers `unsupported`, by words, and its tests skip by them.
# The Windows arm was run on the Windows box (`docs/platforms/windows/`) as the
# commit that wrote this says. systemd's and launchd's protocols (catalogue
# row H14) are the capabilities from `service/notify` on.
#
# The systemd and launchd half (catalogue row H14), each list read on
# 2026-11-23: systemd's own pages `sd_notify(3)`, `sd_listen_fds(3)`,
# `sd_watchdog_enabled(3)` and `sd_booted(3)` (freedesktop.org, the protocol
# `runtime/platform/hero_activation.c` follows); Go's
# `github.com/coreos/go-systemd/v22` `daemon` and `activation` (pkg.go.dev,
# v22.7.0), the `go` lines of this half; Rust's `sd-notify` 0.5.0 and
# `listenfd` 1.0.2 (docs.rs), its `rust` lines; Python's `systemd.daemon` of
# python-systemd (freedesktop.org), its `python` lines, the standard library
# having none of the three. launchd's `launch_activate_socket` is read from
# this Mac's SDK header `launch.h`, which none of the three libraries binds.
# systemd and launchd themselves are unrun here: this Mac has neither a
# systemd host nor a Linux; the protocols run against sockets a program of the
# test makes, and launchd's against a user agent `launchctl` bootstraps.
# On 2026-11-24 the local sockets, FIFOs and special files handed over, the
# pidfd check and the notices of bus services and tuning were run against a
# systemd host: systemd 257 as PID 1 in a Debian 13 container of this Mac's
# Linux arm64 image (kernel 7.0), its socket units handing the descriptors
# and `systemctl show` reading the notices back; and a launchd user agent
# whose socket is a local one, on this Mac.

capability service/start-as-service  the program is the service the Service Control Manager called
  heroes  service.start
  go      svc.Run
  rust    service_dispatcher::start
  python  -
  note    `start` runs the system's dispatcher on a thread of the layer's own and waits for the system to call the program, so `main` keeps the work: Go's `Run` and Rust's `start` block their caller until the service stopped. A program run from a console fails `failed` at once, the words naming the system's refusal, and is told the same again where it asks again: the system's dispatcher is asked once in a process, since its second answer is another error than its first (measured on the box). The system's start limit is 30 seconds, so a longer `limit` never helps. One service a program, once: the own-process kind, the name `sc create` registered, which the system ignores for it

capability service/is-service  whether the system started the program as a service
  heroes  service.is_service
  go      svc.IsWindowsService, svc.IsAnInteractiveSession
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    read from the system: the parent process is the Service Control Manager's and the session is 0, checked on the Windows box; `start` stays the authority. Go's `IsAnInteractiveSession` is deprecated there and is its negation, so it is dropped

capability service/start-parameters  the arguments `sc start <name> <argument>...` gave
  heroes  service.arguments
  go      svc.Handler
  rust    define_windows_service!
  python  -
  note    the parameters after the name, as text, in order; Go's `Execute` is handed them and Rust's `define_windows_service!` parses them into a `Vec<OsString>`

capability service/report-state  the state the program reports to the system, with the controls it accepts
  heroes  service.report_running, service.report_paused, service.report_pending
  go      svc.Status, svc.State, svc.Accepted, svc.Stopped, svc.StartPending, svc.StopPending, svc.Running, svc.ContinuePending, svc.PausePending, svc.Paused, svc.AcceptStop, svc.AcceptShutdown, svc.AcceptPauseAndContinue, svc.AcceptParamChange, svc.AcceptPowerEvent, svc.AcceptSessionChange, svc.AcceptPreShutdown
  rust    ServiceStatusHandle::set_service_status, ServiceStatus, ServiceState, ServiceControlAccept
  python  -
  note    the check point is kept by the layer and moves at each pending report, which the system documents as the sign of progress; a pending state and the stopped state accept no control, as the system asks; the wait hint is up to an hour. `stopped` is reported by `end`, the one report after which the process goes

capability service/end  the service reported stopped, with an exit code
  heroes  service.end
  go      svc.Status
  rust    ServiceExitCode
  python  -
  note    0 for a service that stopped as asked, any other number the service's own code, shown by `sc query` as the service-specific exit code. `end` also lets the system's `ServiceMain` return, so the dispatcher thread ends and the process may exit

capability service/exit-win32-code  a Windows error number reported as the service's exit code
  heroes  pending
  go      svc.Status
  rust    ServiceExitCode
  python  -
  note    pending: Go's `Status.Win32ExitCode` and Rust's `ServiceExitCode::Win32` let a service stop with a system error number, which the system prints in words; `end` takes the service's own code alone

capability service/controls-lifecycle  stop, pause, resume, shutdown and pre-shutdown told to the program
  heroes  service.wait, service.wait_forever
  go      svc.ChangeRequest, svc.Cmd, svc.Stop, svc.Pause, svc.Continue, svc.Shutdown, svc.PreShutdown
  rust    service_control_handler::register, ServiceControl, ServiceControlHandlerResult
  python  -
  note    the handler the system calls runs on the dispatcher's thread, writes the control in a queue of 64 and returns, as the system asks (its reference page: return at once, within 30 seconds); no Heroes code runs on a thread the system made. A control not accepted is answered not implemented, never queued. A full queue loses a notification before a lifecycle control. The stop of the program ends a wait `stopped` after what was queued is told. Go hands a control to a channel and Rust to a closure; Rebel hands a list of values to the program's own `while`

capability service/controls-notifications  parameter, time, power, session and own controls told to the program
  heroes  service.wait
  go      svc.ParamChange, svc.PowerEvent, svc.SessionChange
  rust    ServiceControl, SessionChangeParam, UserEventCode, PowerEventParam
  python  -
  note    each is told with the system's own event number and a session's number, `format_control` naming them; the numbers' meanings (`PBT_`, `WTS_`) are the system's and stand in the face's doc comments. A control of the program's own, 128 to 255, is `user`, its code in `event`

capability service/controls-in-a-set  the service waited on beside connections and timers
  heroes  service.add_to, service.count
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    a datagram socket of the loopback the layer rings while a control waits, which `poll` waits on beside everything else, as `fs/watch` does for its events; a stranger's datagram can make it readable with nothing queued, so a `wait` of zero may answer none. Neither Go nor Rust has a wait of this shape: their controls arrive on a channel or in a closure

capability service/interrogate  the system asking the service to report again
  heroes  refused: the system's own reference page says the handler simply returns NO_ERROR, since the system knows the service's state, so the layer answers it and tells the program nothing; a control told for nobody to act on would only be one more case to match
  go      svc.Interrogate
  rust    ServiceControl
  python  -

capability service/network-bindings  the controls of network bindings
  heroes  refused: the system's reference page for these controls says applications should use Plug and Play instead; the layer sends the system the answer for a control it does not handle
  go      svc.NetBindAdd, svc.NetBindRemove, svc.NetBindEnable, svc.NetBindDisable, svc.AcceptNetBindChange
  rust    ServiceControl, ServiceControlAccept
  python  -

capability service/device-and-hardware-events  device arrivals and hardware profile changes told to the program
  heroes  pending
  go      svc.DeviceEvent, svc.HardwareProfileChange, svc.AcceptHardwareProfileChange
  rust    ServiceControl, HardwareProfileChangeParam
  python  -
  note    pending: the system sends a device event only to a service that registered for it with `RegisterDeviceNotification`, a call this package does not make

capability service/power-settings  a power setting's change told with its value
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    PowerBroadcastSetting, PowerSchemePersonality, MonitorState, DisplayState, AwayModeState, BatterySaverState, LidSwitchStateChange
  python  -
  note    pending: a setting's change reaches a service that registered for it with `RegisterPowerSettingNotification`, and carries the setting's value; `power` tells the event's number alone

capability service/start-reason  why the system started the service
  heroes  pending
  go      svc.StartReason, svc.DynamicStartReason, svc.StartReasonDemand, svc.StartReasonAuto, svc.StartReasonTrigger, svc.StartReasonRestartOnFailure
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    pending: asked of the system with `QueryServiceDynamicInformation`, not written in this step

capability service/manage  services installed, started, stopped, queried and removed
  heroes  pending
  go      svc.ActivityStatus, svc.Active, svc.Inactive, svc.AnyActivity
  rust    Service, ServiceAccess, ServiceAction, ServiceConfig, ServiceFailureActions, ServiceInfo, ServiceType, ServiceStartType, ServiceErrorControl
  python  -
  note    pending: the catalogue's row H15, a service installed (the system's own `sc create` does it by hand today); this package is the program's side of the protocol, a service's running and being controlled

capability service/notify  a datagram of assignments sent to the manager's notice socket
  heroes  service.notify
  go      daemon.SdNotify
  rust    sd_notify::notify
  python  daemon.notify
  note    one datagram of newline-separated assignments to the socket `$NOTIFY_SOCKET` names, a path or on Linux `@` and an abstract name; `vsock:` is `unsupported`. Sent never waiting, so a full queue is `limit`; absent `$NOTIFY_SOCKET` is `ok(false)` as Go's `(false, nil)`. Go, Rust and Python can unset the variable after use and Rebel never writes the environment, so that option is dropped (the manager's own pid check keeps a child's notices from being read as the parent's). Rust's `notify` takes a list of states as this does, Go's and Python's a string

capability service/notify-lifecycle  the program ready, reloading and stopping
  heroes  service.Notice, service.format_notices
  go      daemon.SdNotifyReady, daemon.SdNotifyReloading, daemon.SdNotifyStopping, daemon.SdNotifyMonotonicUsec
  rust    NotifyState
  python  daemon.notify
  note    `ready` is `READY=1`, `stopping` `STOPPING=1`, `reloading` `RELOADING=1` with the `MONOTONIC_USEC=` the page says goes with it, which `notify` writes from `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` (Go's `SdNotifyMonotonicUsec`); the string-building is pure, `format_notices`, so a program tests what it sends

capability service/notify-status  a status line, an errno and a new main process
  heroes  service.Notice
  go      daemon.SdNotify
  rust    NotifyState
  python  daemon.notify
  note    `STATUS=`, `ERRNO=` and `MAINPID=`; a status holds no control character, since a newline would start another assignment (refused `invalid`, never written), an errno is 1 to 4095 and a pid above zero

capability service/notify-watchdog  the watchdog's keep-alive, its trigger, its timeout and a longer startup
  heroes  service.Notice
  go      daemon.SdNotifyWatchdog
  rust    NotifyState
  python  daemon.notify
  note    `WATCHDOG=1`, `WATCHDOG=trigger` (reports an internal error, which the page says acts as a missed ping), `WATCHDOG_USEC=` and `EXTEND_TIMEOUT_USEC=`, each a `time.Duration` written in whole microseconds, a part of one counted as a whole one

capability service/watchdog-timeout  whether a watchdog supervises the program, and its timeout
  heroes  service.watchdog_timeout
  go      daemon.SdWatchdogEnabled
  rust    sd_notify::watchdog_enabled
  python  -
  note    `$WATCHDOG_USEC`, and `$WATCHDOG_PID` where it is set and names another process, which the page says ignores both; where none supervises it is `not_found` (Go answers `(0, nil)`, Rust false), a timeout of 0 `invalid`. The page says to send a keep-alive about every half of the timeout, which is the caller's

capability service/notice-parse  the notices a datagram holds, the supervisor's half
  heroes  service.parse_notices
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    beyond the three libraries, which only send: what a program that supervises another reads of what `notify` sends, and what the tests of `format_notices` read back. A variable no case names is passed over as the manager's own reader passes over what it does not know

capability service/is-booted  whether the system was booted with systemd
  heroes  service.is_booted_by_systemd
  go      -
  rust    sd_notify::booted
  python  daemon.booted
  note    `sd_booted(3)`'s test, that `/run/systemd/system/` exists; Go's `daemon` offers none

capability service/inherit  the sockets systemd handed the program, from descriptor 3
  heroes  service.inherit
  go      activation.Files, activation.FilesWithNames, activation.Listeners, activation.ListenersWithNames, activation.PacketConns
  rust    sd_notify::listen_fds, sd_notify::listen_fds_with_names, ListenFd
  python  daemon.listen_fds, daemon.listen_fds_with_names, daemon.LISTEN_FDS_START
  note    `$LISTEN_PID` must name this process (a child that inherited the variables is handed none, as the page says), `$LISTEN_FDS` is the count, `$LISTEN_FDNAMES` the colon-separated names with `unknown` for none; each descriptor is set close-on-exec as the page says and non-blocking as the socket table needs. Taken once in the life of the process, a second call `exists`: the table owns the descriptors, and one closed and reused by another layer must never be adopted again. The environment is left as set (the unset option is dropped as in `service/notify`)

capability service/inherit-kinds  each descriptor as the value it is
  heroes  service.Handed
  go      activation.Listeners, activation.PacketConns
  rust    ListenFd
  python  daemon.is_socket, daemon.is_socket_inet, daemon.is_fifo
  note    the descriptor is asked what it is, never assumed: a listening TCP socket is a `net.Listener`, a connected one (`Accept=yes`) a `net.Conn`, a UDP socket a `net.UdpSocket`, IPv4 or IPv6, a local socket, a FIFO or a special file the value `service/inherit-local-sockets` names, and anything else is `other`, left open, with words for what it is. Go fills a gap with nil and Python asks before it takes; a value here is never wrong for the descriptor it came from

capability service/inherit-tls  listeners that speak TLS
  heroes  refused: a listener's TLS is the program's own choice at accept, and `tls` wraps the connection it accepts, so a handed socket needs nothing of its own
  go      activation.TLSListeners, activation.TLSListenersWithNames
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    Go forces TLS on each accepted connection from a configuration; here a program takes the listener and wraps each connection it accepts with the `tls` package, the same one way as for a listener it made itself

capability service/inherit-launchd  the sockets launchd hands a job
  heroes  service.inherit_launchd
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `launch_activate_socket(name, &fds, &cnt)` of `launch.h`: the sockets the job's plist names under `Sockets`, handed over once (a second call is `exists`, EALREADY), a program no launchd job runs is `not_found` (ESRCH); each is adopted as systemd's are; more than 64 under one name is `limit`. None of the three libraries binds it

capability service/inherit-local-sockets  Unix-domain sockets, FIFOs and special files handed over
  heroes  service.inherit, service.inherit_launchd, service.Handed
  go      activation.Listeners, activation.PacketConns
  rust    ListenFd
  python  daemon.is_socket_unix, daemon.is_fifo, daemon.is_mq
  note    a local stream socket that listens is `local_listener`, an `ipc.Listener` named by the path or, on Linux, the abstract name it is bound to (launchd binds a local socket in a folder of its own and moves the file to the plist's `SockPathName`, so the name is the path it was bound at), whose close leaves the socket file to the manager that listens on it; one connected (`Accept=yes`) is `local_connection`, an `io.Stream` of `ipc`'s; a FIFO (`ListenFIFO=`) is `fifo` and a character device or a file (`ListenSpecial=`) `special`, each an `io.Stream` read and written as the manager opened it, made blocking as every stream of `io` is. Each descriptor is asked what it is, never assumed, and adopted inside the one call, so a failure closes every value already made. Left `other` with words: a local datagram or sequenced-packet socket (`ipc` has no value for one, its block `ipc/datagrams`), a netlink socket, a POSIX message queue (Linux's file system of them shows one as a regular file, told by its magic), a folder. Run on 2026-11-24 against systemd 257 (`ListenStream=` at a path and at `@` an abstract name, `Accept=yes`, `ListenFIFO=`, `ListenSpecial=/dev/random`, `ListenDatagram=` told `other`) and a launchd user agent's `SockPathName`

capability service/inherit-pidfd  the pidfd check of `$LISTEN_PIDFDID`
  heroes  service.inherit
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `sd_listen_fds(3)` since systemd 259: where `$LISTEN_PIDFDID` is set it must be this process's pidfd inode number, else nothing was handed to this process; a value that is no number of 1 to 20 digits within 64 bits is `invalid`. The number is read as systemd's `pidfd_get_inode_id` reads it: `pidfd_open` on the process, the pidfd file system's magic `PID_FS_MAGIC` checked by `fstatfs`, the inode number by `fstat` on 64 bits (`sd_pidfd_get_inode_id(3)`); where there is none (off Linux, before Linux 6.9, an inode number of 32 bits) the check is passed over, as systemd's `sd_listen_fds` passes it over when its own lookup fails (its `sd-daemon.c`, read on 2026-11-24). None of the three libraries checks it. Run on 2026-11-24 in the systemd container (Linux 7.0) by a launcher that sets `$LISTEN_PIDFDID` and becomes the program by `exec`: its own number handed the listener over, the number plus one handed nothing

capability service/notify-descriptors  descriptors stored with the manager and a barrier
  heroes  refused: a program holds no raw descriptor, and passing one over a local socket (`SCM_RIGHTS`) is a descriptor a Heroes program cannot be trusted to hand over
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  daemon.notify
  note    `FDSTORE=1`, `FDSTOREREMOVE=1`, `FDNAME=`, `FDPOLL=0`, `BARRIER=1` and `MAINPIDFD=1` need descriptors sent beside the datagram; Python's `notify(fds=)` does it. `parse_notices` passes these variables over

capability service/notify-other-variables  the notice variables of bus services and tuning
  heroes  service.Notice.bus_error, service.Notice.varlink_error, service.Notice.exit_status, service.Notice.notify_access, service.Notice.restart_reset, service.NotifyAccess
  go      -
  rust    NotifyState
  python  daemon.notify
  note    `BUSERROR=` (systemd 233) and `VARLINKERROR=` (257) checked against the D-Bus specification's error names and Varlink's interface definition, an empty name taking the error back as the manager does (`empty_to_null` in its `service.c`, read on 2026-11-24); `EXIT_STATUS=` (254) 0 to 255, which the page says the manager keeps as information; `NOTIFYACCESS=` (254) one of `none`, `main`, `exec`, `all` (`systemd.service(5)`); `RESTART_RESET=1` (258). Run on 2026-11-24 against systemd 257, `systemctl show` reading back `StatusBusError`, `StatusVarlinkError` and `NotifyAccess=all`; `RESTART_RESET=1` is newer than it and passed over there, unrun against 258

dropped
  go      svc.Handler (an interface of a callback, which the language has none of: the program's own loop calls `wait`), svc.ChangeRequest.CurrentStatus and .Context (the status and a raw handle of the system), svc.StatusHandle (a raw handle of the system, which the layer keeps)
  rust    define_windows_service! (a macro that writes the entry function a program hands the system: `start` does it), service_control_handler::register's closure and ServiceControlHandlerResult (the handler's return code, which the layer answers), ServiceStatusHandle (a raw token of the system, which the layer keeps), the crate's error types (`Error`, `ParseRawError`: a failure is a `T?` here)
  go      daemon.SdNotifyReady and the other string constants (the cases of `service.Notice`, which cannot be misspelt), activation.TLSListenersWithNames (see `service/inherit-tls`)
  rust    sd_notify::notify_and_unset_env, watchdog_enabled_and_unset_env, listen_fds_and_unset_env, listen_fds_with_names_and_unset_env (the environment is read and never written, README § A5), NotifyState (the cases of `service.Notice`)
  python  daemon._listen_fds, daemon._is_fifo, daemon._is_socket, daemon._is_socket_unix, daemon._is_socket_inet, daemon._is_mq (the descriptor forms of the file-object functions, which a descriptor a program does not hold has no use for), daemon.is_socket_sockaddr (a check of what a socket is bound to, which `net.Listener`'s address tells)
